      "rank": 2,
      "ballot_id": "B017",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Michael Berry",
        "Yakir Aharonov"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The Aharonov–Bohm effect and geometric phase revealed that quantum phase has observable consequences beyond local force fields. These ideas became foundational across quantum mechanics and many areas of physics.",
        "attribution": "Aharonov is central to the Aharonov–Bohm effect, while Berry’s general adiabatic phase concept established a broad and enduring framework.",
        "maturity": "Both effects and their many applications have been extensively confirmed and understood over decades.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The discovery combines distinct contributions from different periods, so the precise scope of a single prize citation would need care."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 3,
      "ballot_id": "B008",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Peter W. Shor",
        "Andrew M. Steane",
        "Alexei Kitaev"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Quantum error correction showed that quantum information can be protected against noise in principle, turning a major conceptual objection into a framework for fault-tolerant computation. Topological codes added a deep connection to phases of matter.",
        "attribution": "Shor and Steane introduced influential early codes, and Kitaev developed the topological-code approach; the proposed set captures distinct foundational contributions.",
        "maturity": "The theory is rigorous and experimentally tested in increasingly capable systems, though large-scale fault-tolerant operation remains unrealized.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The field has many major contributors, and a three-person attribution necessarily omits important work."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B047",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Antoine Georges",
        "Dieter Vollhardt",
        "Gabriel Kotliar"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Dynamical mean-field theory provided a tractable, physically transparent way to understand strongly correlated electrons, including the Mott transition. It is a remarkably useful framework linking analytic ideas with realistic materials.",
        "attribution": "Georges, Vollhardt, and Kotliar are central to developing and establishing the method and its applications.",
        "maturity": "The framework has been extensively developed and applied for decades, with a clear record of explanatory power.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "Its single-site form neglects spatial correlations, so its reach is limited in some low-dimensional systems."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "ballot_id": "B003",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Christophe Salomon",
        "John E. Thomas",
        "Rudolf Grimm"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Realizing strongly interacting Fermi gases and observing the BEC–BCS crossover created a clean experimental setting for universal quantum many-body physics. The work connected previously separated regimes of fermionic superfluidity.",
        "attribution": "The proposed laureates represent major experimental contributions to realizing and studying these gases and the crossover.",
        "maturity": "The systems and central phenomena have been studied extensively, with precise control and a mature body of results.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The discovery and subsequent mapping of the crossover involved several groups, making the boundaries of credit contestable."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 6,
      "ballot_id": "B037",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Juan Maldacena",
        "Edward Witten"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Gauge/gravity duality is a striking conceptual connection between quantum field theory and gravity, with broad influence on theoretical physics and useful tools for strongly coupled systems.",
        "attribution": "Maldacena proposed the concrete duality, and Witten helped clarify and develop its significance; their contributions are especially central to this formulation.",
        "maturity": "The correspondence is mathematically rich and extensively studied, but its physical status as a description of our universe remains untested.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "There is no direct experimental confirmation, and the relation to observable quantum gravity is indirect.",
          "The cited discovery has antecedents in earlier work on string theory and gauge dynamics."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 7,
      "ballot_id": "B010",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Alan H. Guth",
        "Andrei Linde",
        "Viatcheslav Mukhanov"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Inflation offers an influential explanation for the early universe’s large-scale uniformity and the origin of primordial structure. The link between quantum fluctuations and observed cosmological structure is physically compelling.",
        "attribution": "Guth introduced the inflationary idea, Linde developed important models, and Mukhanov made foundational contributions to the theory of primordial perturbations.",
        "maturity": "The theory’s perturbation predictions are well integrated into modern cosmology, but inflation itself has not been uniquely established by observation.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "Many inflationary models fit current data, and a decisive signature distinguishing them has not been observed."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 8,
      "ballot_id": "B028",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Marc Mézard",
        "Riccardo Zecchina"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The cavity method and message-passing theory brought statistical physics to random constraint-satisfaction problems and exposed rich glassy transitions in computation. This is a notable conceptual bridge between physics and computer science.",
        "attribution": "Mézard and Zecchina are strongly associated with the development and demonstration of these methods.",
        "maturity": "The theoretical framework and its influence are well established, though its status is more specialized than that of discoveries with broad experimental reach.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The method’s strongest results rely on assumptions about random ensembles that do not always carry over to practical instances."
        ]
      }
    }
